we see that quadratic probing was solve the cluster problem in linear probing,
but he could miss some indexes (we could improve it but not solve it)
double hashing is a function that solve this
he is not create cluster!
he is not miss indexes!

newIndex = (h(k) + i * h2(k)) % m

h2(k) : another independent hash function
        the most useful one is
        h2(k) = prime - (k % prime)

Note: prime is a prime number (3,4,5,7,11,...)
      that is smaller than table size (m)
      there is some complex mathematics under the hood
      but this function works very well!


imagine our hash table use a Array with length of 5
and we use double hashing for resolve the collisions
(our hash function is h(k) = k % m)
[,,,,]

h(k)  = k % m

h2(k) = 3-( k % 3 )    (3 is a prime number smaller than 5)


insert k=6,v=A
               h(6) = 6%5 = 1
               [,(6,A),,,]

insert k=8,v=B
               h(8) = 8%5 = 3
               [,(6,A),,(8,B),]

insert k=11,v=c
               h(11) = 11%5 = 1
               the index of 1 is full!
               h2(11) = 3-(11 % 3 )= 1
               newIndex = (h(k) + i * h2(k)) % m
                        = (1 + 1 * 1) % 5           i=1
                        = 2
               [,(6,A),(11,C),(8,B),]

Note: this algorithm will spread the  new indexes
      so he will prevent clusters.
      and he will not miss any indexes!
